  "result": "Time dependent loss reweighting for flow matching and diffusion models is theoretically justified (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.16599v1)\nciteturn6752view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn6742view3\",\"lineno\":207}); Total lines: 814\nL194: noting that the second integral in the first equality vanishes, since $w(t)=0$ for every $t\\in A\\cap\\{w(t)=0\\}$ . Now, through the bound $\\mathcal{D}(A\\cap\\{w(t)>n_{0}^{-1}\\})>0$, we obtain\nL195:  | $\\displaystyle\\int_{A}w(t)\\mathcal{D}(dt)=\\int_{A\\cap\\{w(t)>0\\}}w(t)\\mathcal{D}(dt)$  | $\\displaystyle\\geq\\int_{A\\cap\\{w(t)>n_{0}^{-1}\\}}w(t)\\mathcal{D}(dt)$  |\nL196:  |  | $\\displaystyle\\geq\\int_{A\\cap\\{w(t)>n_{0}^{-1}\\}}n_{0}^{-1}\\mathcal{D}(dt)$  |\nL197:  |  | $\\displaystyle=n_{0}^{-1}\\mathcal{D}(A\\cap\\{w(t)>n_{0}^{-1}\\})$  |\nL198:  |  | $\\displaystyle>0,$  |\nL199: which shows that $\\int_{A}w(t)\\mathcal{D}(dt)>0$ whenever $\\lambda(A)>0$. Let $K:=\\int_{[0,1]}w(t)\\mathcal{D}(dt)$. Then since $\\lambda([0,1])=1>0$, it follows that $K>0$. Also note that $K<\\infty$, since by assumption $\\int w(t)\\mathcal{D}(dt)=\\mathbb{E}_{t\\sim\\mathcal{D}}[w(t)]<\\infty.$ Hence,\nL200: \nL201:  | $$\\widetilde{\\mathcal{D}}(A)=K^{-1}\\int_{A}w(t)\\mathcal{D}(dt)>0$$  |\nL202: \nL203: for all $A\\subset[0,1]$ with $\\lambda(A)>0$. Hence, $\\widetilde{D}\\gg\\lambda$, and we conclude by noting that\nL204:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathbb{E}_{t\\sim\\widetilde{\\mathcal{D}}}[f(t)]=\\int f(t)\\widetilde{\\mathcal{D}}(dt)$  | $\\displaystyle=K^{-1}\\int f(t)w(t)\\mathcal{D}(dt)$  |\nL205:  |  | $\\displaystyle=K^{-1}\\cdot\\mathbb{E}_{t\\sim\\mathcal{D}}[w(t)f(t)].$  |\nL206: \nL207: ∎\nL208: ###### Theorem 4.8.\nL209: \nL210: Let $L_{\\mathrm{gm}}(\\theta)$ be the generator matching loss defined in Section cite11†4.2 ,\nL211: \nL212:  | $$L_{\\mathrm{gm}}(\\theta)=\\mathbb{E}_{t\\sim\\mathcal{D},X_{t}\\sim p_{t}(dx)}[w(t)D_{t,X_{t}}(F_{t}(X_{t}),F_{t}^{\\theta}(X_{t}))]$$  |\nL213: \nL214: where the time distribution $\\mathcal{D}$ dominates the Lebesgue measure $\\lambda$, i.e. $\\mathcal{D}\\gg\\lambda$, and the weighting function $w(t)$ is positive $\\lambda$-almost everywhere. Suppose that\nL215: \nL216:  | $$L_{\\mathrm{gm}}(\\theta)=0$$  |\nL217: and that the GM regularity assumptions listed in Appendix cite21†C hold for $\\mathcal{L}_{t}$ and $\\mathcal{L}_{t}^{\\theta}$, for the same class of test functions $\\mathcal{T}$. Then the model parametrized generator $\\mathcal{L}_{t}^{\\theta}$ solves the Kolmogorov Forward Equation (KFE) for $p_{t}$ on $[0,1]$, and the Markov process $X_{t}^{\\theta}$ associated to $\\mathcal{L}_{t}^{\\theta}$ with the initial distribution $p_{0}$ will satisfy $X_{1}^{\\theta}\\sim p_{1}(dx)$.\nL218: ###### Proof.\nL219: \nL220: Since $\\mathcal{D}\\gg\\lambda$ and $w(t)>0$ for $\\lambda$-almost every $t\\in[0,1]$, we use Lemma cite45†4.7 to obtain $\\widetilde{D}\\gg\\lambda$ and a constant $K>0$ such that\nL221:  | $\\displaystyle L_{\\mathrm{gm}}(\\theta)$  | $\\displaystyle=\\mathbb{E}_{t\\sim\\mathcal{D},X_{t}\\sim p_{t}(dx)}[w(t)D_{t,X_{t}}(F_{t}(X_{t}),F_{t}^{\\theta}(X_{t}))]$  |\nL222:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\mathbb{E}_{t\\sim\\mathcal{D}}[w(t)\\mathbb{E}_{X_{t}\\sim p_{t}(dx)}[D_{t,X_{t}}(F_{t}(X_{t}),F_{t}^{\\theta}(X_{t}))]$  |\nL223:  |  | $\\displaystyle=K^{-1}\\cdot\\mathbb{E}_{t\\sim\\widetilde{\\mathcal{D}}}[\\mathbb{E}_{X_{t}\\sim p_{t}(dx)}[D_{t,X_{t}}(F_{t}(X_{t}),F_{t}^{\\theta}(X_{t}))]$  |\nL224:  |  | $\\displaystyle=K^{-1}\\cdot\\mathbb{E}_{t\\sim\\widetilde{D},X_{t}\\sim p_{t}(dx)}[D_{t,X_{t}}(F_{t}(X_{t}),F_{t}^{\\theta}(X_{t}))].$  |\nL225: Since rescaling the loss by a positive constant factor simply corresponds to a different choice of Bregman divergence (cf. Example cite46†B.1 ), it therefore suffices to only consider the case $w(t)\\equiv 1$ and $\\mathcal{D}\\gg\\lambda$ in the proof going forward.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTime dependent loss reweighting for flow matching and diffusion models is theoretically justified (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.16599v1)\nciteturn6752view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn6742view3\",\"lineno\":95}); Total lines: 814\nL83:  | $$\\mathcal{L}_{t}f(x)=\\langle\\mathcal{K}_{t,x}f,\\ F_{t}(x)\\rangle_{V_{t,x}},$$  |\nL84: \nL85: for a function $F_{t}:S\\to\\bigsqcup_{x\\in S}\\Omega_{t,x}$ such that $F_{t}(x)\\in\\Omega_{t,x}$ for every time $t$ and $x\\in S$. If such a construction exists, then $F_{t}$ is said to linearly parametrize $W_{t}$, or with the appropriate context, to linearly parametrize the generator $\\mathcal{L}_{t}$.\nL86: Assume that for each $z\\in\\mathcal{Z}$, where $\\mathcal{Z}$ is a latent conditioning space, the conditional Markov process $X_{t}^{z}$ has a conditional infinitesimal generator $\\mathcal{L}_{t}^{z}$. Then a time and state varying linear parametrization of $\\mathcal{L}_{t}^{z}$ is given by\nL87: \nL88:  | $$\\mathcal{L}_{t}^{z}f(x)=\\langle\\mathcal{K}_{t,x}f,\\ F_{t}^{z}(x)\\rangle_{V_{t,x}},$$  |\nL89: where $F_{t}^{z}:S\\to\\bigsqcup_{x\\in S}\\Omega_{t,x}$ is such that $F_{t}^{z}(x)\\in\\Omega_{t,x}$ for every time $t$, $x\\in S$ and $z\\in\\mathcal{Z}$, and we require the following regularity conditions:\nL90: \nL91:   1. 1.\nL92: \nL93: For each time $t\\in[0,1]$ and $x\\in S$, it holds that $\\dim(V_{t,x})<\\infty$ and\nL94: \nL95:  | $$\\mathbb{E}_{Z\\sim p_{Z|t}(dz|x)}\\|F_{t}^{Z}(x)\\|_{V_{t,x}}<\\infty,$$  |\nL96: \nL97: where $p_{Z|t}(dz|x)$ is the posterior distribution $(Z|X_{t}=x)$.\nL98: \nL99:   2. 2.\nL100: The GM regularity assumptions listed in Appendix cite21†C hold for the marginal and model parametrized generators and their associated Markov processes. For the model parametrized generators, we write\nL101: \nL102:  | $$\\mathcal{L}_{t}^{\\theta}f(x)=\\langle\\mathcal{K}_{t,x}f,F_{t}^{\\theta}(x)\\rangle_{V_{t,x}},$$  |\nL103: \nL104: and we denote $X_{t}^{\\theta}$ for the associated Markov process. For the marginal generator, we write\nL105: \nL106:  | $$\\mathcal{L}_{t}f(x)=\\langle\\mathcal{K}_{t,x}f,F_{t}(x)\\rangle_{V_{t,x}}$$  |\nL107: and simply denote $X_{t}$ for the associated Markov process. See Theorem cite37†4.11 for a connection between the conditional and marginal parametrizations.\nL108: ###### Remark 4.1.\nL109: \nL110: We believe that the theory mentioned here can be extended to when, e.g., $V_{t,x}$ is a Hilbert space, and that in this case similar conditions hold. However, for simplicity, we consider only when $\\dim V_{t,x}<\\infty$, because we need to parametrize the vectors in $V_{t,x}$ by a neural network $F_{t}^{\\theta}(x)$.\nL111: ### 4.2 Time and state varying loss\nL112: \nL113: In this section, we extend the concept of a generator matching loss (GM loss) and a conditional generator matching loss (CGM loss) as introduced in GM and FMG, to allow for a general class of time distributions $\\mathcal{D}$, with an extended class of Bregman divergences $D_{t,x}$ that depend on both time and state, applied to linear parameterizations that are time and state dependent.\nL114: As in e.g. cite38†Banerjee et al. (2005) and cite39†Della Pietra et al. (2002) , we consider Bregman divergences corresponding to $\\phi:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb{R}$ that might only differentiable on the relative interior of $\\Omega$, denoted $\\mathrm{ri}(\\Omega)$, which is defined as the interior of $\\Omega$ taken within its affine hull (see Remark cite40†A.2 ). This is in contrast to requiring differentiability on all of $\\Omega$.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLlamazip: Leveraging LLaMA for Lossless Text Compression and Training Dataset Detection (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.17589v1)\nciteturn6752view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn6742view2\",\"lineno\":230}); Total lines: 406\nL227: Table cite73†5 (bpc) in Appendix cite38†B displays the number of bits per character required by different text compression algorithms depending on the document. Concerning the enwik9 document, the 10 batches of 100,000 tokens each are concatenated.\nL228: FSDFSD, MIT1 and MIT2 are the three documents that were published after the release date of LLaMA3 and thus, are known to not be in the training set. On average, Llamazip requires 2.0693 bits per character. On a document that is likely not part of the underlying LLaMA training dataset, the original authors of LLMZip found that their algorithm compresses a document using 0.8426 bits per character.\nL229: This shows that recording the rank of the prediction rather than recording the token itself if the prediction is wrong is a more effective strategy for compressing the data. However, the work done on LLMZip has focused solely on text compression, whereas the present study finds that Llamazip might detect if a document is part of the training set of an LLM.\nL230: ### 5.4 Speed and Memory Requirements\nL231: \nL232: Table cite74†6 in Appendix cite38†B displays the number of tokens compressed per second depending on the quantization and context window. The compression speed is perfectly negatively correlated with the window size, with a Spearman’s $\\rho$ of -1.\nL233: Modern hardware has dedicated float32 accelerators, which can even perform two float16 operations in the same time frame required by a single float32 operation. However, the NVidia 4090, which at the time of writing is the highest-end NVidia consumer-grade Deep Learning accelerator, does not have any int4 or int8 accelerators.\nL234: Llamazip compresses data at least as fast using float16 and bfloat16 quantizations compared with integer quantizations at equal context window sizes, while offering a better compression ratio.\nL235: The memory requirements displayed in Table cite75†7 in Appendix cite38†B are based on the memory usage obtained using the command “nvidia-smi”, an official program provided by NVidia. Consistent with expectations, both quantizations and the size of the context window have a positive impact on the quantity of memory used during compression, since an int4 parameter requires 4 times fewer bits than a float16 one and the context window has to be entirely kept in memory to predict the next token.\nL236: As expected, bfloat16 and float16 require the exact same amount of memory, since both formats use 16 bits per parameter. Each larger quantization size, even with the smallest context window, requires more memory than the smaller size with the longest context window.\nL237: ## 6 Identifying Documents in the Training Dataset\nL238: \nL239: The performance of the Llamazip algorithm varies widely depending on the document being compressed, with previously seen data being compressed more than data that are outside of the training set.\nL240: The hypothesis proposed here is that the observed difference in compression ratios between the highest-performing and lowest-performing parameters of Llamazip, depending on whether the document is present in the underlying training set, is attributed to the model’s memorisation of the document. By intentionally limiting the model’s capacity to recall its training set, through a reduction in parameter precision, its access to memory is diminished.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEvoVLA: Self-Evolving Vision-Language-Action Model (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.16166v1)\nciteturn6752view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn6742view1\",\"lineno\":149}); Total lines: 570\nL146: We instead treat memory as context: at each step we select Top-$K$ history items as independent context tokens, fuse them with the current latent via a learned gate, and perform lightweight write-back with utility-guided eviction (usage, recency, redundancy) rather than adjacent averaging. This retains phase-specific information while keeping the store compact.\nL147: Context Selection via Attention. We extract a latent representation $x_{t}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}$ from the OpenVLA-OFT backbone at each step (by pooling over the LLM’s hidden states), and we maintain a memory store $\\mathcal{M}=\\{m_{i}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}\\}_{i=1}^{L}$ with timestamps $\\{\\tau_{i}\\}$, where $L$ is the store capacity. To select relevant history, we define query, key, and value projections:\nL148:  | $\\displaystyle q_{t}$  | $\\displaystyle=W_{q}x_{t},$  |  | (9)\nL149:  | $\\displaystyle k_{i}$  | $\\displaystyle=W_{k}\\!\\big(m_{i}+\\text{PE}(\\tau_{i})\\big),$  |\nL150:  | $\\displaystyle v_{i}$  | $\\displaystyle=W_{v}m_{i},$  |\nL151: \nL152: where $W_{q},W_{k},W_{v}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d\\times d}$ are learnable linear maps, and $\\text{PE}(\\cdot)$ is a sinusoidal temporal positional encoding. Attention scores and the selected index set are computed as:\nL153:  | $\\displaystyle a_{i}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\frac{\\exp(\\langle q_{t},k_{i}\\rangle/\\sqrt{d})}{\\sum_{j=1}^{L}\\exp(\\langle q_{t},k_{j}\\rangle/\\sqrt{d})},$  |  | (10)\nL154:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathcal{S}_{t}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\text{TopK}\\big(\\{a_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{L},K\\big),$  |\nL155: \nL156: where $K$ selects the most relevant history items. We prepend $\\{v_{i}\\mid i\\in\\mathcal{S}_{t}\\}$ to the current sequence as independent context tokens, replacing static adjacent-averaging consolidation used in prior work.\nL157: Gated Fusion and Reward Modulation. Let $\\hat{h}_{t}=\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal{S}_{t}}a_{i}v_{i}$ be the weighted context embedding. We fuse it with the current latent $x_{t}$ via a learned gate:\nL158: \nL159:  | $\\displaystyle g^{\\text{mem}}_{t}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\sigma\\!\\big(w_{g}^{\\top}[\\hat{h}_{t};x_{t}]\\big),$  |  | (11)\nL160:  | $\\displaystyle\\tilde{x}_{t}$  | $\\displaystyle=(1-g^{\\text{mem}}_{t})x_{t}+g^{\\text{mem}}_{t}\\hat{h}_{t}.$  |\nL161: where $w_{g}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{2d}$ is a learnable weight vector and $[\\cdot;\\cdot]$ denotes concatenation. The gate $g^{\\text{mem}}_{t}$ modulates how much history context influences the current representation. The memory gate also modulates the base progress reward $r^{\\text{base}}_{t}$:\nL162: \nL163:  | $$r^{\\text{prog}}_{t}=g^{\\text{mem}}_{t}\\cdot r^{\\text{base}}_{t}.$$  |  | (12)\nL164: suppressing spurious progress signals when history context indicates unstable manipulation patterns (e.g., repeated failures, oscillations). We block gradients through $g^{\\text{mem}}_{t-1}$ to prevent reward hacking. Memory update writes back the fused representation $\\tilde{x}_{t}$ only to the selected items $\\mathcal{S}_{t}$, and eviction removes the least useful entry based on a utility score combining usage frequency, recency, and redundancy with existing items.\nL165: POE Integration and Effect. In contrast to Titans-MAC [cite106†3 ] and MemoryVLA [cite105†45 ], Long-Horizon Memory uses selective context with gated fusion for reward-level modulation. The core mechanism operates on backbone latents $x_{t}$ and history items $m_{i}$ via the attention and gating mechanisms above. When combined with POE (full model, Table cite107†2 row 5 vs.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSolving Spatial Supersensing Without Spatial Supersensing (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.16655v1)\nciteturn6752view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 12; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn6748view3\",\"pattern\":\"Feedback\"}); Total lines: 537\nL373@P7-8: 8admissible baselines can saturate key tasks without performing any long-horizon spatial integration. A genuine first\nL374@P8: step toward supersensing requires demonstrating at least one capability that trivial retrieval-based methods cannot\nL375@P8: achieve. Until then, we should view performance of future methods on VSI-Super with caution for claims about\nL376@P8: spatial supersensing or long-horizon world modeling.\nL377@P8: Going forward, we view world-model evaluation as an important research problem, alongside world-modeling itself.\nL378@P8: Progress in spatial supersensing will require benchmarks that are robust to trivial transformations, explicitly require\nL379@P8: revisits to previous states and long-horizon integration, along with routinely stress-testing with adversarial baselines\nL380@P8: and perturbations. Our analyses move in this direction by exposing the fragility of current evaluations and by\nL381@P8: pointing to concrete, more faithful tests of video world models.\nL382@P8: Limitations\nL383@P8: Our analysis has two main limitations. Empirically, we study a single benchmark family (VSI-Super) and one\nL384@P8: primary method (Cambrian-S). Other datasets or algorithms may behave differently, so our conclusions about\nL385@P8: spatial supersensing may not generalize. Conceptually, we diagnose failure modes and propose design principles, but\nL386@P8: we do not introduce a validated replacement benchmark. Building such benchmarks remains an open challenge and\nL387@P8: will require iterative cycles of task design, adversarial probing, and model development.\nL388@P8: Acknowledgments\nL389@P8: The authors would like to thank (in no particular order): Sebastian Dziadzio, Adhiraj Ghosh, Shashwat Goel,\nL390@P8: Matthias K¨ummerer, Thao Nguyen, and Ronald Skorobogat for helpful feedback and comments on our draft. We\nL391@P8: thank the authors of Cambrian-S for engaging with our work and for their constructive feedback. This work is\nL392@P8: funded by the Federal Ministry of Education and Research (BMBF), FKZ: 01IS24079A. VU was supported by\nL393@P8: a Google PhD Fellowship in Machine Intelligence. VU also thanks the European Laboratory for Learning and\nL394@P8: Intelligent Systems (ELLIS) PhD program for support. AH and VU thank the International Max Planck Research\nL395@P8: School for Intelligent Systems (IMPRS-IS) for support. AH acknowledges funding by the Federal Ministry of\nL396@P8: Education and Research (BMBF), FKZ: 01IS24079A. AP and MB acknowledge acknowledges financial support\nL397@P8: by the Federal Ministry of Research, Technology and Space (BMFTR), FKZ: 16IS24085B and Open Philanthropy\nL398@P8: Foundation funded by the Good Ventures Foundation. This research utilized compute resources at the T¨ubingen\nL399@P8: Machine Learning Cloud, DFG FKZ INST 37/1057-1 FUGG.\nL400@P8: References\nL401@P8: Nishant Balepur, Rachel Rudinger, and Jordan Lee Boyd-Graber. Which of these best describes multiple choice evaluation\nL402@P8: with llms? a) forced b) flawed c) fixable d) all of the above. arXiv preprint arXiv:2502.14127, 2025. 8\nL403@P8: Lucas Beyer, Olivier J H´enaff, Alexander Kolesnikov, Xiaohua Zhai, and A¨aron van den Oord. Are we done with imagenet?\nL404@P8: arXiv preprint arXiv:2006.07159, 2020. 8\nL405@P8: Ellis Brown, Jihan Yang, Shusheng Yang, Rob Fergus, and Saining Xie. Benchmark designers should” train on the test set”\nL406@P8: to expose exploitable non-visual shortcuts. arXiv preprint arXiv:2511.04655, 2025. 2\nL407@P8: Shyamal Buch, Crist´obal Eyzaguirre, Adrien Gaidon, Jiajun Wu, Li Fei-Fei, and Juan Carlos Niebles. Revisiting the” video”\nL408@P8: in video-language understanding. In Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF conference on computer vision and pattern recognition,\nL409@P8: pages 2917–2927, 2022. 2\nL410@P8: Nikhil Chandak, Shashwat Goel, Ameya Prabhu, Moritz Hardt, and Jonas Geiping. Answer matching outperforms multiple\nL411@P8: choice for language model evaluation. arXiv preprint arXiv:2507.02856, 2025. 8\nL412@P8: Mostafa Dehghani, Yi Tay, Alexey A Gritsenko, Zhe Zhao, Neil Houlsby, Fernando Diaz, Donald Metzler, and Oriol Vinyals.\nL413@P8: The benchmark lottery. arXiv preprint arXiv:2107.07002, 2021. 2\nL414@P8: Robert Geirhos, J¨orn-Henrik Jacobsen, Claudio Michaelis, Richard Zemel, Wieland Brendel, Matthias Bethge, and Felix A\nL415@P8: Wichmann. Shortcut learning in deep neural networks. Nature Machine Intelligence, 2020. 2\nL416@P8-9: 9Aryo Pradipta Gema, Joshua Ong Jun Leang, Giwon Hong, Alessio Devoto, Alberto Carlo Maria Mancino, Rohit Saxena,\nL417@P9: Xuanli He, Yu Zhao, Xiaotang Du, Mohammad Reza Ghasemi Madani, et al. Are we done with mmlu? In Proceedings of\nL418@P9: the 2025 Conference of the Nations of the Americas Chapter of the Association for Computational Linguistics: Human\nL419@P9: Language Technologies (Volume 1: Long Papers), pages 5069–5096, 2025. 8\nL420@P9: Adhiraj Ghosh, Sebastian Dziadzio, Ameya Prabhu, Vishaal Udandarao, Samuel Albanie, and Matthias Bethge. Onebench\nL421@P9: to test them all: Sample-level benchmarking over open-ended capabilities. In Proceedings of the 63rd Annual Meeting of\nL422@P9: the Association for Computational Linguistics (Volume 1: Long Papers), pages 32445–32481, 2025. 8\nL423@P9: Yash Goyal, Tejas Khot, Douglas Summers-Stay, Dhruv Batra, and Devi Parikh. Making the v in vqa matter: Elevating the\nL482@P10: Mert Yuksekgonul, Federico Bianchi, Pratyusha Kalluri, Dan Jurafsky, and James Zou. When and why vision-language\nL483@P10: models behave like bag-of-words models, and what to do about it? arXiv preprint arXiv:2210.01936, 2022. 5\nL484@P10: Xiaohua Zhai, Basil Mustafa, Alexander Kolesnikov, and Lucas Beyer. Sigmoid loss for language image pre-training. In\nL485@P10: Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF International Conference on Computer Vision, pages 11975–11986, 2023. 3\nL486@P10: Haoji Zhang, Yiqin Wang, Yansong Tang, Yong Liu, Jiashi Feng, Jifeng Dai, and Xiaojie Jin. Flash-vstream: Memory-based\nL487@P10: real-time understanding for long video streams. arXiv preprint arXiv:2406.08085, 2024. 5\nL488@P10: Daniil Zverev, Thadd¨aus Wiedemer, Ameya Prabhu, Matthias Bethge, Wieland Brendel, and A Koepke. Vggsounder:\nL489@P10: Audio-visual evaluations for foundation models. In Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF International Conference on Computer\nL490@P10: Vision, pages 1027–1037, 2025. 8\nL491@P10-11: 11A Feedback from Cambrian-S Authors\nL492@P11: We aim to foster open discussion in the spirit of journals such as Behavioral and Brain Sciences (BBS), which use\nL493@P11: an open peer commentary format for controversial work. Before releasing this work, we emailed the Cambrian-S\nL494@P11: authors to share our findings. We present their response to give readers a more balanced perspective on this work:\nL495@P11: Response from the Cambrian-S authors (November 20th, 2025)\nL496@P11: Thank you very much for contacting us and sharing your findings! We truly appreciate the attention you have given\nL497@P11: to our work. Below, we have answered your questions, giving you our different views and some personal thoughts.\nL498@P11: After thoroughly reading your manuscript, we find your experimental settings to be sound. Some of your test setups\nL499@P11: are exactly what we had in mind! We appreciate your effort in designing this simple yet strong solution and sharing\nL500@P11: it with us.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLlamazip: Leveraging LLaMA for Lossless Text Compression and Training Dataset Detection (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.17589v1)\nciteturn6752view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn6742view2\",\"pattern\":\"training data\"}); Total lines: 406\nL271: However, this task is made more difficult by the relative obscurity of the datasets used to train LLMs and the constant increase in the distribution of machine-generated content, which is not always labelled as such.\nL272: This last point is of particular importance and projects such as Wordfreq cite77†Speer (2013) , which is a database of word frequencies for various natural languages, will not be updated by its author since generative models have “polluted” the data, which is no longer representative of human usage. For example, it has been observed cite78†Shapira (2024) that ChatGPT overuses the word “delve” compared to humans.\nL273: ## 8 Limitations\nL274: \nL275: Although Llamazip demonstrates promising results in lossless text compression and dataset provenance detection, it has several limitations that must be acknowledged:\nL276: ### 8.1 Uncertainty Regarding Training Dataset\nL277: The effectiveness of Llamazip in compressing certain documents may be influenced by whether those documents were part of LLaMA3’s original training set. However, due to the lack of transparency in the construction of LLaMA3’s dataset and the computational challenges associated with analyzing such a large-scale corpus, the authors cannot definitively confirm whether texts such as Alice’s Adventures in Wonderland (alice29) and Frankenstein were included in LLaMA3’s training data.\nL278: This uncertainty introduces potential biases in compression performance that could impact our findings.\nL279: ### 8.2 Potential Contamination in Post-LLaMA3 Texts\nL280: The authors assumes that documents published after the release of LLaMA3 were not included in its training data. However, this assumption is imperfect, as many contemporary texts, even those published after LLaMA3’s release, may have been generated or edited with the assistance of large language models.\nL281: Given the increasing prevalence of AI-assisted writing, it is possible that some of these documents contain stylistic or linguistic patterns that are already well-represented in LLaMA3’s training data, leading to unexpectedly high compression rates. This complicates the task of identifying whether a document was truly unseen by the model.\nL282: ### 8.3 Language Scope Restriction\nL283: \nL284: The current study is limited to English-language text, as LLaMA3 was primarily trained on English data. Consequently, Llamazip’s ability to compress non-English text remains unexplored. Since linguistic structures, tokenization schemes, and model memorization behaviors differ across languages, extending Llamazip to multilingual datasets may require significant modifications and further evaluation.\nL285: ## 9 Conclusion\nL286: In conclusion, this work has demonstrated the multifaceted potential of Llamazip as a novel, lossless text compression algorithm that leverages the predictive capacity of LLaMA3. By only storing the tokens that the model fails to predict accurately, Llamazip achieves significant compression, optimising storage requirements without compromising data integrity.\nL287: This approach to compression trades processing speed for maximal data reduction, making it well-suited for archival purposes where storage efficiency takes precedence over rapid decompression.\nL288: Beyond its primary function in data compression, Llamazip introduces an innovative capability to verify whether specific documents were included in the training set of an LLM. By comparing its compression performance under various parameter settings, a discernible pattern emerges that differentiates known documents from previously unseen ones.\nL289: This property positions Llamazip as a promising tool for identifying the provenance of data within an LLM training context, a crucial capability to address concerns about data privacy, intellectual property, and transparency of training sources.\nL290: Finally, this word has also explored the effects of model quantization and context window size on Llamazip’s performance, highlighting how these variables influence next-token prediction accuracy and, consequently, compression efficiency.\nL291: ## 10 Acknowledgments\nL292: \nL293: This publication has emanated from research conducted with the financial support of Science Foundation Ireland under Grant number 18/CRT/6183. For the purpose of Open Access, the author has applied a CC BY public copyright licence to any Author Accepted Manuscript version arising from this submission.\nL294: ## References\nL295:   * Arnold and Bell (1997) R. Arnold and T. Bell A corpus for the evaluation of lossless compression algorithms. In Proceedings DCC’97. Data Compression Conference, pp. 201–210. Cited by: cite79†§3.2 .\nL296:   * asweigart (2012) asweigart Codebreaker. GitHub. Note: cite80†https://github.com/asweigart/codebreaker†github.com Cited by: cite81†§3.3 .\nL297:   * Bellard (2019) F. Bellard Lossless data compression with neural networks. Bellard. Note: cite82†https://bellard.org/nncp/nncp.pdf†bellard.org Cited by: cite83†§2 .\nL298:   * Fenniak (2006) M. Fenniak Pypdf. GitHub. Note: cite84†https://github.com/py-pdf/pypdf†github.com Cited by: cite85†§3.7 .\nL299:   * for Transformative Works (2007) O. for Transformative Works Archive of our own. Archive of Our Own. Note: cite86†https://archiveofourown.org†archiveofourown.org Cited by: cite87†§3.4 .\n"
